<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flemish Baroque painting</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1585–<a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flemish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> painting is the art of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-netherlands">Southern Netherlands</a> from the late sixteenth to the late seventeenth century, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens">Peter Paul Rubens</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-van-dyck">Anthony van Dyck</a>, Jacob Jordaens and Frans Snyders, it produced large altarpieces for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> churches, portraits, still lifes and landscapes. Rubens's workshop and the city's art market made Antwerp one of the main artistic centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flemish-baroque-painting.jpg" alt="Flemish Baroque painting" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_De_kruisoprichting.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The period is usually taken to begin with the Spanish recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1585, which split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> regions to the south, and to end about 1700, when Spanish Habsburg authority ended with the death of King Charles II. In this usage Flemish often includes regions outside modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, such as the Duchy of Brabant and the Prince-Bishopric of Liège. Late Mannerist painters such as Otto van Veen, Adam van Noort, Marten de Vos and the Francken family prepared the ground, making many altarpieces to replace those destroyed in the iconoclastic outbreaks of 1566.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens">Peter Paul Rubens</a>, a student of both van Veen and van Noort, spent eight years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1600 to 1608, studying classical antiquity, the Italian Renaissance, Adam Elsheimer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> he set up a studio that trained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-van-dyck">Anthony van Dyck</a>. After his death in 1640 Jacob Jordaens became the most important painter of Antwerp, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, the seat of the court, drew David Teniers the Younger later in the century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Painters tended to specialise, and independent masters often worked together: Rubens usually painted the figures, while Frans Snyders added animals and Jan Brueghel the Elder landscapes and plants. History painting, held by theorists of the time to be the noblest art, was led by Rubens, whose Descent from the Cross triptych of 1611–1614 in the Cathedral of Our Lady in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> ideas about art to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> naturalism, dynamism and monumentality.</p><p>Several genres were partly Flemish innovations. Jan Brueghel the Elder developed the flower still life around 1600 and, with Cardinal Federico Borromeo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, the flower garland around an image of the Virgin and Child. Rubens and Snyders painted monumental hunting scenes, Frans Francken the Younger and Willem van Haecht gallery paintings of art collections, and Adriaen Brouwer small interiors of peasants fighting and drinking; at his death Rubens owned more works by Brouwer than by any other painter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Van Dyck became court painter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a> and was influential on later English portraiture, and the way Jordaens treated his monumental genre subjects inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> artists such as Jan Steen. Brueghel's flower compositions influenced later Dutch flower pieces. The small cabinet paintings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-netherlands">Southern Netherlands</a> were traded widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, and the gallery paintings, interpreted as a kind of visual theory of art, foreshadowed the Italian veduta and the galleries of Giovanni Paolo Pannini.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Flemish Baroque painting” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…sustained much of the art of the Low Countries: Rogier van der Weyden was city painter of Brussels, Dieric Bouts worked in Leuven, Pieter Bruegel the Elder lived in Antwerp and Brussels, and Peter Paul Rubens made Antwerp the centre of <b>Flemish Baroque painting</b> in the seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
